+# Release blocked: `libfoo-1.4.2.tar.gz` does not match `SHA256SUMS`
+
+## What is blocked
+
+`python3 verify.py` still exits 1 after fixing the verifier, because one release
+artifact is genuinely corrupt (or the manifest entry for it is wrong):
+
+```
+$ shasum -a 256 artifacts/libfoo-1.4.2.tar.gz
+fde9b2575edbefb297fa7d93738b4bb1f09b7f777d1723b90194ad53cd9bc9ce  artifacts/libfoo-1.4.2.tar.gz
+
+$ grep libfoo SHA256SUMS
+7BEE020AC21EFB3831D920AF0B8C5EFE8D44CDF227AB94847F509188420356BD  libfoo-1.4.2.tar.gz
+```
+
+The other two artifacts match their manifest entries.
+
+## Why it cannot be fixed here
+
+The original bytes of `libfoo-1.4.2.tar.gz` cannot be reconstructed from this
+repository: there is only one commit (`init`) containing the current file, and
+no backup, patch, or second copy of the expected content. No verifier change can
+make a SHA-256 mismatch disappear without disabling or weakening the check.
+
+This is not worked around by editing `SHA256SUMS` or replacing the artifact,
+because either would publish bits that were never validated against the release
+manifest.
+
+## Fix already applied
+
+`verify.py` had two real bugs, now fixed:
+
+1. It opened artifacts in text mode (`open(path)`) and hashed `a.read().encode()`.
+   That decodes binary bytes using the platform encoding and re-encodes them,
+   which either raises `UnicodeDecodeError` or hashes different bytes than the
+   file contains. It now reads in binary mode (`open(path, "rb")`).
+2. It compared the computed lowercase hex digest against the manifest entry
+   verbatim. The release manifest uses uppercase hex (and so does the CI test),
+   so every entry failed. The comparison is now case-insensitive.
+
+After this fix:
+
+```
+$ python3.15 -m pytest test_verify.py -q
+.F.  1 failed, 2 passed
+FAILED test_verify.py::VerifyTests::test_release_artifacts_verify
+AssertionError: Lists differ: ['libfoo-1.4.2.tar.gz'] != []
+```
+
+`test_binary_files` and `test_reports_missing` pass; the only failing check is
+the one asserting all release artifacts verify, and it fails solely because of
+the `libfoo` mismatch above.
+
+## What is needed to unblock
+
+Obtain either:
+
+- a fresh, intact `libfoo-1.4.2.tar.gz` whose SHA-256 is
+  `7BEE020AC21EFB3831D920AF0B8C5EFE8D44CDF227AB94847F509188420356BD`, or
+- confirmation from the release owner that the manifest entry should be
+  `fde9b2575edbefb297fa7d93738b4bb1f09b7f777d1723b90194ad53cd9bc9ce`,
+  together with the provenance of the current file.
+
+Until one of those is provided, the release check cannot legitimately pass.
